# quanta-memory-platform — Cursor Rules

## Architecture & Crate Boundaries

`quanta-memory-platform` is a portable, host-independent memory budgeting /
accounting workspace. It is consumed by the Semantica monorepo through a host
adapter (`quanta-memory-host`) that lives **outside this repo**, and it is
designed to be extracted into its own git repository with no rewrites. The crate
graph is the architecture authority.

Allowed workspace edges (strict, acyclic — `core <- facade <- testkit`):

```text
quanta-memory-platform-core    -> (none — pure)
quanta-memory-platform-facade  -> quanta-memory-platform-core
quanta-memory-platform-testkit -> quanta-memory-platform-core, quanta-memory-platform-facade
quanta-memory-host (Semantica) -> quanta-memory-platform-{core,facade}   # OUTSIDE this repo
```

No member depends upward. `core` must not name `facade`/`testkit`; `facade` must
not name `testkit`. The host adapter never appears as a dependency of this repo —
that would invert the direction.

Layout:

```text
crates/
  quanta-memory-platform-core/     # pure hexagonal kernel
    src/api/         # public broker surface: broker, builder, control
    src/application/ # use-case services: controller (pressure), scheduler
    src/domain/      # config, context, errors, ledger (accounting SSOT), policy, stats
    src/ports/       # trait definitions: pin, scratch, store, telemetry
    src/adapters/    # port impls: broker-backed scratch, hdrhistogram, metrics; explicit unbudgeted pin
  quanta-memory-platform-facade/   # feature-sliced portable stores + pipelines
    src/{payload_store,retained_map,revocable_store,transcript_store,publish_pipeline,spill}/
    src/{presets,shared}/
  quanta-memory-platform-testkit/  # conformance / chaos / alloc tooling (never a prod dep)
    src/{conformance,faults,fixtures,doctor,fake,alloc_tracking}/
```

Boundary rules (hexagonal direction is inward-only):

- **`domain/`** is pure: no I/O, no `std::fs`, no `std::env`, no wall-clock
  (`SystemTime::now` / `Instant::now`), and it must NOT import `crate::adapters`.
  It depends on its own types plus the `ports/` trait shapes.
- **`ports/`** holds trait definitions only — no concrete adapters, no I/O.
- **`adapters/`** implement port traits; infra types (hdrhistogram, metrics) live
  here. Allocator telemetry from adapters is **advisory**: it never mutates ledger
  counters. The in-memory pin registry is explicitly named `UnbudgetedPinRegistry`
  and exists only behind the non-default `unbudgeted_pin` feature.
- **`application/`** orchestrates domain + ports; carries no host nouns.
- **`api/`** is the public broker surface, builder-first. Low-level broker
  backdoors stay `pub(crate)` / unexported.
- **`facade`** composes core behind builders; production storage backings such
  as redb are feature-gated and confined to their `*/backings/` modules.
- **`testkit`** is NEVER a mandatory production dependency. `alloc_tracking` (the
  only `tracking-allocator` integration) is feature-gated so it can never enter a
  production graph.

Forbidden usages (fail-closed under `-D warnings` + the repo-local rail + deny):

- Semantica nouns in the public API (`Semantica*`, `quanta_runtime`, `codegraph`).
  The portable library carries none.
- `core` depending on `facade`/`testkit`; `facade` depending on `testkit`.
- A path dependency escaping the workspace (breaks extraction).
- `crate::adapters` imported from `domain/` or `ports/`.
- Raw constructor backdoors leaking past the builder-first surface; public API
  compile-fail contracts and review own this boundary.

Enforcement: the root workspace lint policy, `cargo clippy --workspace
--all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings`, `cargo deny --all-features check`,
`scripts/check-workspace.sh`, and the extraction pre-flight in
`scripts/extract-to-standalone-repo.sh`. The shared Cargo-metadata checker owns
member presence, resolved dependency direction, host back-edges, and local path
dependencies escaping the workspace; it catches renamed dependencies without
treating comments as edges. The extractor also owns the self-hosting file set
and exact committed-tree export. If a change needs a NEW crate edge, stop and
reconsider — do not add the import to make it compile.

## Rule Cheatsheet (self-check before the gate)

The **Enforced by** column is the authority. Semgrep rows and compiler/Clippy
rows are machine-enforced by `scripts/check-workspace.sh`; rows marked `review`
are review contracts, not automated gates. Do not claim a review-only rule is
fail-closed. A Semgrep exception requires a trailing
`// nosemgrep: <rule-id> -- reason: <…>`.

| Don't write this | Write this instead | Enforced by |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `x.unwrap()` in non-test code | `x.ok_or_else(|| MemoryError::config("…"))?` | review |
| `panic!` / `unreachable!` / `todo!` / `unimplemented!` in shipping paths | return a `MemoryError` variant | review |
| `.expect("")` / `.expect("TODO")` | only in `#[cfg(test)]`, with `.expect("<why this cannot fail>")` | review |
| `Err(_) => {}` / `let _ = fallible();` / `.ok();` | propagate with `?` or map to a typed `MemoryError` | review |
| `map_err(|_| …)` when the source carries actionable context | preserve it: `map_err(|e| MemoryError::config(format!("…: {e}")))`; discarding poison or range-conversion details is allowed when the replacement error is complete | review |
| bare `#[allow(<lint>)]` (no reason) | append `-- reason: <…>` on the same line | review |
| `arr[i]` / `&s[a..b]` on untrusted indices | `arr.get(i)` / checked slicing | review |
| `n as u64` (lossy / truncating) | `u64::try_from(n)?` or a checked/saturating op | review |
| `x as i64` / `as i32` / `as i16` / `as i8` (sign-flips a magnitude — the `bytes as i64` ledger-corruption bug) | keep `u64` magnitudes with saturating/checked ops; `i64::try_from(..)?`; widen a signed *diff* to `i128` | regression-guards (`qmp-magnitude-as-signed`) |
| `a.saturating_mul(b) / c` (saturates BEFORE the divide → under-reports the ratio: the pressure-percent / weighted-cap bug) | `u64::try_from(u128::from(a) * u128::from(b) / u128::from(c))` | regression-guards (`qmp-saturating-mul-then-divide`) |
| `Atomic::fetch_sub(..)` on a counter (can underflow → wraps to MAX: the spilled-count drift) | saturating CAS: `c.fetch_update(SeqCst, SeqCst, \|v\| Some(v.saturating_sub(n)))` | regression-guards (`qmp-atomic-fetch-sub`) |
| wrapping arithmetic on byte/budget counters | `checked_add` / `saturating_sub` per the documented invariant | review (ledger correctness) |
| live reconfigure / policy swap that re-checks only future reserves, not the CURRENT resident usage (e.g. raising `headroom_bytes` over live non-Headroom bytes) | re-validate the live snapshot against the NEW policy and fail-closed before installing it | review (ledger invariant 5) |
| byte-target sweep/reclaim loop that breaks only on `reclaimed >= target` | also handle zero-contribution entries / exhaustion so a positive target can't over-evict (skip zero-byte entries) | review |
| durable-queue FIFO key taken from an atomic counter OUTSIDE the write txn | derive the next key from the table INSIDE the write transaction (atomic, restart-safe, no skipped/duplicate keys) | review (spill backing) |
| `a == b` on floats | epsilon / ordered comparison | clippy `float_cmp` |
| `dbg!(...)` | remove it | clippy `dbg_macro` |
| `std::fs` / `std::env` / `SystemTime::now` / `Instant::now` in `domain/` or `ports/` | route through a port + adapter; keep domain pure | regression-guards (`qmp-pure-layer-no-fs-env`, `qmp-pure-layer-no-wall-clock`) |
| `crate::adapters` imported from `domain/` or `ports/` | depend on the port trait only | regression-guards (`qmp-pure-layer-no-adapter-import`) |
| resolved dependency from `core` to facade/testkit (or facade to testkit), including a renamed dependency | keep `core <- facade <- testkit` | Cargo-metadata graph contract in `check-workspace`, extraction pre-flight |
| Semantica nouns (`Semantica*`, `quanta_runtime`, `codegraph`) in public API | keep the library host-independent | review |
| raw broker constructor backdoor in public code | go through the builder-first surface | public API compile-fail tests |
| stale `V1`/`V2` / old renamed symbols | use the current names | review |
| allocator telemetry mutating ledger counters | telemetry is **advisory** — never writes counters | review (ledger invariant) |
| mutating the ledger partway then erroring | validate first; fail-closed BEFORE any mutation | review (all-or-nothing) |
| `assert!(true)` / `assert_eq!(x, x)` placeholder test | assert the real behavior / invariant | review |
| `#[ignore]` without a reason | `#[ignore = "<reason>"]` | review |

Run the full rail with `scripts/check-workspace.sh`.

## Build & Verification Front Door

Plain `cargo` is the build front door. `scripts/check-workspace.sh` is the only
repository-wide verification front door. It owns formatting, ShellCheck,
resolved workspace-graph and safety policy, Python and extraction contracts,
Semgrep regression guards, the Cargo feature matrix, workspace tests, isolated
feature capability tests, documented selector validation, generated-guidance
drift, and dependency policy.

```bash
scripts/check-workspace.sh
```

Run focused commands while developing, but do not report them as equivalent to
the canonical rail. The feature matrix discovers feature names from Cargo
metadata and covers default, bare, every non-aggregate feature, `full`, and
all-features; adding a manifest feature therefore adds it to the matrix without
editing a shell list.

Examples use the real public APIs:

```bash
cargo run -p quanta-memory-platform-facade --example hello_world
cargo run -p quanta-memory-platform-facade --example effective_budget_report
```

Generated guidance:

```bash
python3 tools/pm/pm.py sync
python3 tools/pm/pm.py lint
python3 tools/pm/pm.py status
python3 -m unittest -q tools/pm/tests/test_pm.py
```

The prompt manager is Python-standard-library-only. Its JSON manifest rejects
orphan sources, duplicate outputs, path escapes, missing outputs, and drift.
The Rust toolchain version is owned solely by `rust-toolchain.toml`; dependency
policy is owned by `deny.toml`; formatting is owned by `rustfmt.toml`. Workspace
Rust/Clippy lint levels are owned by the root `Cargo.toml`; keep project-wide
lints there instead of repeating crate attributes.

Extraction is mechanical and fail-closed:

```bash
scripts/extract-to-standalone-repo.sh --dry-run /path/to/dest   # pre-flight only
scripts/extract-to-standalone-repo.sh /path/to/dest             # copy + verify + commit
```

## Test Topology

Tests must **catch real regressions** in budget accounting, policy resolution,
pressure/scheduling, and the portable stores — not merely import modules.

Placement:

| If it proves… | Put it in… |
| --- | --- |
| Ledger reserve/commit/release + its declared invariants | `crates/quanta-memory-platform-core/src/domain/ledger.rs` `#[cfg(test)]` |
| Policy / manifest / preset / residency validation | `crates/quanta-memory-platform-core/src/domain/{policy,config}.rs` `#[cfg(test)]` |
| Pressure controller / scheduler behavior | `crates/quanta-memory-platform-core/src/application/{controller,scheduler}.rs` `#[cfg(test)]` |
| Public broker surface end-to-end (reserve→commit→snapshot→release→rebuild) | `crates/quanta-memory-platform-core/src/api/control.rs` `#[cfg(test)]` |
| Cross-cutting policy contracts | `crates/quanta-memory-platform-core/tests/policy_contracts.rs` |
| A facade store / pipeline behavior | that feature's `service.rs` `#[cfg(test)]` |
| A storage backing (redb) round-trip | the backing module under its feature |
| "the conformance suite actually bites" | `testkit` conformance + `self_tests` |
| Deterministic fault/chaos scenarios | `crates/quanta-memory-platform-testkit/src/faults/` |

Conventions:

- **Conformance must bite.** The testkit's meta-requirement: feed a deliberately
  broken/lying fake into the public suite and assert it is REJECTED, alongside the
  honest-fake and real-broker positive cases. A suite that passes everything is
  worthless — preserve this when extending.
- **Assert invariants, not vibes.** After an op, assert the ledger invariants
  (`total_owner_bytes`, class/owner balance) hold — not just that a call returned
  `Ok`. No `assert!(true)` / `assert_eq!(x, x)` placeholders.
- **Assert on the `MemoryError` variant**, not on stringly-typed message
  substrings where avoidable.
- **Feature-gate tests** with the feature they exercise; `--all-features` must be
  green and each feature must build alone.
- `#[ignore]` needs a reason: `#[ignore = "<why>"]`.

Run before closeout:

```bash
scripts/check-workspace.sh
```

Regression guards (`scripts/regression-guards.sh`, rules in
`tools/semgrep/qmp-regressions.yml`) pin bug classes this repo already hit —
magnitude-to-signed casts, saturate-before-divide ratios, underflowing
`fetch_sub`, and direct I/O/clock/adapter access from pure layers. A
flagged-but-safe use needs a trailing
`// nosemgrep: <rule-id> -- reason: <…>`.

## Determinism & Accounting Invariants

The ledger (`crates/quanta-memory-platform-core/src/domain/ledger.rs`) is the
semantic accounting SSOT. It is
single-threaded and synchronous; the public broker wraps it behind a lock. Its
behavior must be **deterministic and reproducible** for the same operation
sequence — conformance suites and fault fixtures depend on it.

Global invariants (must hold after every committed operation):

1. `sum(class usage) == sum(owner usage)`
2. `sum(owner usage) == retained payload bytes + retained metadata bytes
   + live scratch reservations`  (the `ChargeKind` partition)
3. `reserved <= soft <= hard`  (validated when the policy is installed)
4. No live writes while `rebuild_in_progress`

Rules:

- **Fail-closed before mutation.** Validate the whole operation first; every
  mutating op either fully succeeds or leaves the ledger untouched. Never mutate
  partway and then return `Err`.
- **Telemetry is advisory.** Allocator telemetry (hdrhistogram/metrics adapters)
  never mutates ledger counters — it only observes.
- **Stable ordering.** Anything iterated for a snapshot, report, or assertion uses
  ordered containers (`BTreeMap`) or an explicit sort. Do not depend on `HashMap`
  iteration order for snapshots, `LedgerSnapshot`, `EffectiveBudgetReport`, or
  conformance output.
- **Deterministic fixtures.** `testkit` fault/chaos scenarios are deterministic —
  no timing heuristics, no wall-clock, no RNG driving outcomes. A scenario that
  "sometimes" fails is a bug.
- **Checked arithmetic on counters.** Byte/budget math uses `checked_*` /
  `saturating_*` per the documented invariant — never silent wrap or lossy `as`.

### No silent heuristics

- **Override resolution:** the effective-budget resolver layers overrides by an
  explicit, documented precedence (`OverrideSource`); do not infer precedence
  from map order.
- **Capacity decisions:** an over-budget reserve returns `MemoryError::Capacity`,
  not a silently clamped or partial reservation.
- **Conformance must bite:** a suite that passes everything is worthless — the
  testkit proves it REJECTS a broken/lying fake as well as accepting an honest
  one. Keep that property when extending it.

## Error Handling

A memory-accounting platform that **drops failures quietly** corrupts the ledger.
Fail loudly and fail-closed.

The single error type is `MemoryError`
(`crates/quanta-memory-platform-core/src/domain/errors.rs`):

```text
MemoryError = Build | Config | Capacity | InvalidState | Report | Unsupported
MemoryResult<T> = Result<T, MemoryError>
```

Domain-intent aliases all resolve to `MemoryError`: `CapacityError`, `PinError`,
`PublishError`, `StoreWriteError`, `StoreDeleteError`. Construct via the helpers
(`MemoryError::config("…")`, `::capacity("…")`, `::build("…")`, `::report("…")`).

Principles:

- **No silent recovery.** Production code must not coerce a capacity / config /
  invalid-state failure into an empty default or a swallowed `Ok`.
- **Fail-closed before mutation.** An over-budget reserve, an invalid policy, or a
  write during rebuild returns the appropriate `MemoryError` BEFORE touching the
  ledger — never half-apply then error.
- **Preserve the source.** When wrapping a backing error (redb), include
  it: `format!("spill write failed: {e}")`. Never `map_err(|_| …)`.
- **Pick the right variant.** Over-budget → `Capacity`; bad manifest/policy →
  `Config`; builder misuse → `Build`; operation illegal in current state (e.g.
  write during rebuild) → `InvalidState`; snapshot/report failure → `Report`;
  not-wired capability → `Unsupported`.

Rust patterns:

| Avoid | Prefer |
| --- | --- |
| `.unwrap()` in non-test code | `?` with a `MemoryError` variant, or `.ok_or_else(...)` |
| `.expect("")` / `.expect("TODO")` | tests only, with `.expect("<invariant that cannot fail>")` |
| `Err(_) => {}` / `let _ = fallible();` | propagate with `?` or map to a typed error |
| `panic!()` / `todo!()` in shipping paths | return `MemoryError` with an explicit variant |
| `map_err(|_| MemoryError::…)` | `map_err(|e| MemoryError::…(format!("…: {e}")))` |
| lossy `as` / wrapping math on counters | `try_from` + `checked_*` / `saturating_*` |

`MemoryError` derives `Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq` and implements
`std::error::Error` + `Display` — assert on the variant in tests, not on string
substrings where avoidable.

## Dependency Policy

Runtime dependencies are declared in each crate's `Cargo.toml`, pinned in the
root `[workspace.dependencies]`, and audited by `cargo deny --all-features check`
against `deny.toml`.

**Shared versions** (pin in root `[workspace.dependencies]`, opt in per crate):

- `redb` — facade `spill` backing
- `hdrhistogram`, `metrics` — core `telemetry` adapters
- `tracking-allocator` — testkit `alloc_tracking` ONLY

**Rules:**

- Every non-trivial third-party crate is **optional** and behind a feature gate.
  `core` and `facade` build with `--no-default-features` and pull infra crates in
  only via their feature (`telemetry`, `spill`, `revocable_store`, …).
- `tracking-allocator` may appear ONLY under testkit `alloc_tracking`; it must
  never reach a production dependency graph.
- New runtime deps must pass `cargo deny --all-features check`. `deny.toml`
  allows only licenses actually encountered in the `--all-features` graph, so an
  unused allowance warns and a newly introduced license fails closed. If you add
  a dep with a new license, add it to the allow-list with a one-line rationale.
- Stay inside the architecture edges (`core <- facade <- testkit`). Do not add a
  dependency that pulls in Semantica/host crates — that inverts the direction.
- No dependency may introduce a path dep escaping the workspace (breaks
  extraction). Inter-member deps stay relative within `crates/`.

`deny.toml` notes: private unpublished members are license-exempt and duplicate
versions fail. MPL-2.0 is accepted only for the testkit tracking-allocator path.
Revisit that allowance before any distributed binary release.
